{
  "id": "pc-applications-05",
  "version": 1,
  "title": "Complex numbers in polar form",
  "xp": 25,
  "tags": [
    "precalculus",
    "complex-numbers",
    "polar"
  ],
  "learn": [
    {
      "id": "intro",
      "kind": "text",
      "title": "Complex numbers as points",
      "body": "A complex number $a+bi$ is the point $(a,b)$ on a plane. Its modulus is $r=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}$ and its argument $\\theta$ is the angle. Polar form is $z=r(\\cos\\theta+i\\sin\\theta)$."
    },
    {
      "id": "p1",
      "kind": "example",
      "title": "Write $1+i$ in polar form",
      "body": "Find $r$ and $\\theta$.",
      "steps": [
        "$r=\\sqrt{1+1}=\\sqrt2$",
        "$\\theta=\\tan^{-1}1=45^\\circ$",
        "$1+i=\\sqrt2(\\cos45^\\circ+i\\sin45^\\circ)$"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p2",
      "kind": "text",
      "title": "Multiplying is easy",
      "body": "To multiply two complex numbers in polar form, multiply the moduli and add the angles. For $2$ at $30^\\circ$ times $3$ at $60^\\circ$: modulus $6$, angle $90^\\circ$, so the product is $6i$."
    },
    {
      "id": "p3",
      "kind": "example",
      "title": "Powers of $1+i$",
      "body": "Use the polar form.",
      "steps": [
        "$(1+i)^2$: modulus $(\\sqrt2)^2=2$, angle $90^\\circ$, so $2i$",
        "$(1+i)^8$: modulus $(\\sqrt2)^8=16$, angle $360^\\circ$",
        "So $(1+i)^8=16$"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p4",
      "kind": "tip",
      "body": "In general $z^n=r^n(\\cos n\\theta+i\\sin n\\theta)$. Powers scale the length and multiply the angle."
    }
  ],
  "questions": [
